21:45:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There may be average differences in preferences or in what people notice, but “men are naturally wired not to care about clutter” is too broad to treat as a fact. Individual variation is huge, and habits, standards, workload, and household roles matter a lot. Also, noticing clutter is partly a learned skill: if one person has routinely been responsible for anticipating guests, finding lost items, keeping shared spaces usable, and doing the cleaning, they tend to become more attuned to mess. Someone who has not had to manage those consequences may genuinely notice it later—not because they are incapable of noticing, but because their attention and routines developed differently. It’s fair to say you’ve encountered several messy men. It’s less fair—or useful—to assume any particular man will be messy because he’s a man.
