21:45:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Testosterone and biology may influence some average behavioral tendencies, but there isn’t strong basis for treating testosterone as the simple, primary explanation for whether someone notices clutter or keeps a home tidy. Tidiness is a mix of attention, habits, executive function, available time, stress, standards, storage, and—especially in shared homes—whether someone has learned they’re responsible for the cleanup. Social roles aren’t an abstract add-on: they affect who gets practice noticing household tasks and who faces consequences when they’re ignored. It’s fine to say that, in your experience, men you’ve lived with were messier. But “men are naturally wired to be slobs because of testosterone” goes beyond what that experience—and the evidence—can reliably show.
